{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Prevention remediation for incident 1c0d — DECIDED as a bug\n\nPost-mortem: `9097-2741-6462-424c`. Many agent sessions ran `review-plan` concurrently on ONE shared Bedrock account, self-inflicting account-level throttling. The 1c0d fix made throttling *retryable*; this hardens the retry/backoff so shared-account clients de-synchronize instead of forming a thundering herd.\n\n### Decision (operator-approved 2026-08-29): harden the existing escalating backoff, not a limiter\n\nA client-side concurrency limiter was **declined**: it duplicates the provider-side rate limit (a moving target). Escalating backoff is reactive to the actual throttle signal, needing no knowledge of the global limit. OSS research (6 pydantic-ai consumers + vendor SDKs) confirms the shape — see the decision comment.\n\n### Scope (two parts, both in the Anthropic transport at `src/rebar/llm/anthropic_model.py`, `_build_retrying_anthropic_provider`)\n\n1. **Jitter the fallback.** Today `wait_retry_after(fallback_strategy=None, ...)` resolves to pydantic-ai's default **non-jittered** `wait_exponential(multiplier=1, max=60)`. When Anthropic returns no `Retry-After` under a shared-account 429, N clients back off by the *same* deterministic amount and retry in lockstep. Replace the fallback with jitter — `random.uniform(cap/2, cap)` over an exponential cap, per gobifrost/bifrost (this formula is **Equal Jitter** in Marc Brooker's AWS taxonomy) — so concurrent clients scatter. This restores parity with botocore adaptive mode (our Bedrock path, already jittered) and with the vendor SDK jitter we opted out of via SDK `max_retries=0`.\n2. **Guard zero/expired `Retry-After`.** pydantic-ai's `wait_retry_after` honors `retry_after=0` (or an already-expired HTTP-date) as \"wait zero\" → a synchronized *immediate* replay across all shared clients — a second, independent herd vector. Treat a non-positive / expired `Retry-After` as ABSENT and fall through to the jittered fallback (bifrost's `_wait_for_retry` guard).\n\n### Why a bug, not an ADR\nStandard-correctness (\"backoff must jitter to avoid synchronized retry storms\" — AWS guidance), disposition-preserving (still retryable), consistent with the transport-retry epic (jira-reb-687). No design decision remains open.\n\n### Out of scope\nA shared-account throttle-rate observability metric/alert is a separate, lower-priority follow-up. No client-side limiter (declined).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Anthropic transport fallback wait applies Equal Jitter `uniform(cap/2, cap)` (not plain `wait_exponential`); a held-out test asserts two synthesized concurrent 429-without-Retry-After retries draw *different* sleeps (de-correlation), bounded by `llm_retry_max_wait_s`.\n- [x] A `Retry-After: 0` (and expired HTTP-date) is treated as absent → jittered fallback, NOT immediate replay; test asserts sleep > 0 within the jitter window.\n- [x] A positive `Retry-After` within `llm_retry_max_wait_s` is still honored (regression guard).\n- [x] `make lint && make typecheck && make test` green (held-out jitter oracle + regression suite; ruff + mypy + pytest).\n- [x] [non-codebase] Lands via Gerrit (LLM-Review +1 AND Verified +1) — change 2448 MERGED; see the 'Landed' comment for run/vote artifacts.\n      provenance: environment=github-actions-verified-lane + gerrit-review-bot; principal=rebar-ci-bot + rebar-review-bot; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — Gerrit change 2448 merged to origin/main; both labels are bot-cast merge gates.\n\n## Implementation plan (Phase 2 — confirmed against installed pydantic-ai 1.107.2)\nRuntime-confirmed current behavior of `wait_retry_after(fallback_strategy=None, max_wait=60)`:\nno-`Retry-After` fallback → deterministic `1.0` (identical across clients → lockstep);\n`Retry-After: 0` → `0.0` (immediate replay); `-5` → `-5.0`; positive `5` → `5.0`; expired\nHTTP-date falls to the same non-jittered fallback.\n\nExtract a module-level factory `_build_retry_wait(*, max_wait, rng=None) -> Callable[[RetryCallState], float]`\nin `anthropic_model.py` (call-graph seam off `_build_retrying_anthropic_provider`):\n1. **Jittered fallback** — the gobifrost/bifrost formula the decision approved,\n   `cap = min(2 ** (attempt_number - 1), max_wait)`, `sleep = rng.uniform(cap / 2, cap)`. Bounded\n   by `llm_retry_max_wait_s`; always `> 0` for `cap > 0`; replaces pydantic-ai's non-jittered\n   `wait_exponential(multiplier=1, max=60)` default.\n2. **Zero/expired `Retry-After` guard** — before delegating to `wait_retry_after` for the\n   positive-honor path, intercept a non-positive integer `Retry-After` (`<= 0`) or an\n   already-past HTTP-date and treat it as ABSENT → jittered fallback (never an immediate replay).\n   Positive in-window `Retry-After` is still delegated/honored (capped at `max_wait`).\n\n**Production RNG source (de-correlation basis).** With `rng=None` the factory binds the\n**process-global `random` module**, which Python seeds from OS entropy once per interpreter start.\nThe herd is N *separate agent processes* each running one `review-plan` on the shared account, so\nthey draw from independent, differently-seeded streams — that per-process independence is what\nde-correlates their retries. `rng` is a **test-only injection seam** (a seeded `random.Random`),\nnever threaded from production config. Bedrock (botocore adaptive) path untouched — Anthropic only.\n\n### Test plan (held-out RED→GREEN, per test-design standard)\nNew file `tests/unit/test_transport_retry_jitter.py` — in-process unit tests against the real\nwait strategy (pure-calculation seam); assert the **observable computed wait value**, never a\nprivate name.\n- **AC1 de-correlation** (`dynamic` observable): seeded `random.Random`; two draws at the same\n  `attempt_number` with no `Retry-After` differ AND both ∈ `[cap/2, cap]`; contrast control: a\n  non-jittered `wait_exponential` yields the identical value both times.\n- **AC2 zero/expired guard**: `Retry-After: 0`, `-5`, and an expired HTTP-date each yield a wait\n  `> 0` within the jitter window (not `0.0`).\n- **AC3 regression**: positive `Retry-After: 5` (≤ `max_wait`) returns `5.0`; a value above\n  `max_wait` is capped at `max_wait`.\n- **Mutation check**: re-introducing the non-jittered fallback OR removing the guard returns the\n  held-out test to RED.\n\n**Impact on existing `tests/unit/test_transport_retry.py`.** The zero-guard deliberately changes\nwhat `Retry-After: 0` does (that IS the fix), so the five tests that injected it as a\n*make-retries-instant* lever are re-pointed to a **zero-backoff config**\n(`_cfg(llm_retry_max_wait_s=0)` → `cap=0` → jitter `0.0`); they assert retry **mechanics** (counts,\nself-heal, logging), never timing, so this is a contract change, not weakening. Separately the\n`test_500_...` fallback timing test (no `Retry-After`) moves from `>= 0.9s` to the Equal-Jitter\nwindow `[cap/2, cap]`.\n\n## Testing\n- Held-out RED→GREEN: `pytest tests/unit/test_transport_retry_jitter.py -q` (asserts the observable computed wait — de-correlation, zero/expired guard, positive-honor/cap).\n- Regression: `pytest tests/unit/test_transport_retry.py -q` (mechanics at zero-backoff config; timing test to the jitter window).\n- Full gate: `make lint && make typecheck && make test`; lands via Gerrit (LLM-Review +1 AND Verified +1).\n\n### Root cause & attribution\nTwo thundering-herd vectors: (A) non-jittered fallback (identical `1.0s` across clients) and (B)\n`Retry-After: 0`/negative/expired honored as immediate synchronized replay. Origin: story\n`morbid-uncultured-arcticduck` (commit `434dc0a`, epic jira-reb-687) first wired the retry with\n`wait_retry_after(fallback_strategy=None)` and no zero-guard — `caused_by` recorded; close class\n`preexisting`."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788137100213298253,"uuid":"f2fe0be3-dc41-4467-8ff6-edb703226a8b"}